1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,750
Far away, a source sends out waves: radio waves, from a galaxy or a black hole.

2
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:09,050
Two radio telescopes on the ground both receive them.

3
00:00:09,050 --> 00:00:15,450
But the source isn't straight overhead, so each wavefront reaches one dish first, and the other a moment later.

4
00:00:15,450 --> 00:00:17,300
That moment is the delay.

5
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:22,475
It depends on where the source is in the sky, and it changes slowly as the Earth turns.

6
00:00:23,375 --> 00:00:26,050
A radio telescope doesn't take a picture.

7
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:30,450
It records the voltage from its receiver, millions of times a second.

8
00:00:30,450 --> 00:00:33,612
Most of that voltage is the receiver's own noise.

9
00:00:33,612 --> 00:00:40,162
The source is a faint pattern hidden in both recordings, the same pattern in each, shifted by the delay.

10
00:00:40,162 --> 00:00:42,200
By eye, you can't see it.

11
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:45,975
To find it, slide one recording past the other.

12
00:00:45,975 --> 00:00:50,250
At each shift, multiply them sample by sample, and add up the products.

13
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:54,900
Mostly, the products are as often negative as positive, and the sum stays small.

14
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:59,612
When the hidden patterns line up, the products turn mostly positive, and the sum jumps.

15
00:00:59,612 --> 00:01:01,925
That curve is the cross-correlation.

16
00:01:01,925 --> 00:01:03,887
Its peak sits at the delay.

17
00:01:03,887 --> 00:01:08,450
xBeam computes it this way, through Fourier transforms, on the GPU.

18
00:01:09,350 --> 00:01:14,550
Radio astronomers correlate recordings from dishes thousands of kilometres apart.

19
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:23,913
Lined up by their delays, each pair of dishes, a baseline, sees detail as fine as a single dish as wide as the distance between them.

20
00:01:23,913 --> 00:01:28,300
That's how the Event Horizon Telescope made its images of black holes.

21
00:01:29,175 --> 00:01:37,712
The same idea works for any two receivers that hear one source: two microphones, or the two detectors that heard the first gravitational wave.

22
00:01:37,712 --> 00:01:45,088
In this module, you'll find a delay you know, correlate three radio stations, and measure that wave's delay yourself.

23
00:01:45,088 --> 00:01:47,975
Each time, the correlation hands you a number.

24
00:01:47,975 --> 00:01:50,100
The question is when to believe it.
